Subject: [07/14] tools: interface-model-free TBR_eff between fixed planes
 settles the estimator question

Adds tools/tbr_plane.py: TBR_eff = [T(-84 A) - T(+84 A)] / q, interpolated from
the binned profile, with no assumption about where the interfaces are or how
wide they are.

Results (v1.6 protocol, 150 ps):
  run_v038 flat 1 eV/ps : 1.3567e-8 +- 5.2 %
  run_v040 flat 3 eV/ps : 1.3569e-8 +- 4.5 %   <- identical, true linear response
  run_v039 P350 1 eV/ps : 1.4455e-8 +- 5.0 %
  run_v041 P350 3 eV/ps : 1.3796e-8 +- 4.6 %

Three consequences:
1. Linear response is confirmed to 0.01 % (flat, 1 vs 3 eV/ps), far tighter than
   the 13 % agreement seen with window extrapolation.
2. The window extrapolation in postprocess.py is 2.6x LOW for this geometry: the
   plane method and the global piecewise fit (1.38e-8) agree, so every historical
   TBR in this repository carries that systematic, including the 2.5e-8 quoted in
   the stage 0-2 report.
3. The patterned P350 is 1.7-6.6 % ABOVE flat, i.e. grooves do not reduce TBR.
   The earlier 'P350 is best (0.60x)' screening result does not survive.

The residual 22 % half-to-half spread of TBR_eff (4.5 sigma) means the interface
is still slowly evolving; runs run_v042/v043 (1.6 ns) will tell whether it is a
trend or a plateau.
